A: I noticed that you are comparing index of x before and after removing, inserting or setting operation. If you are looking for properties to be maintained as they were post an operation, they should be identical except the count. 
Since you have observed that the array list count changes post insertion, removal or setting, we can assume that count is not changing and properties which we are comparing are correct as they are meant to be.
This might be a wrong assertion, but it could be valid if you are looking for the case where no changes happen.
It should also be pointed out that PEX's contract test tool is primarily focused on finding bugs in the system but it doesn't provide a comprehensive suite of tests (like unit testing). Unit tests should be used for verifying each individual function. But, contract testing can verify that certain operations preserve certain properties.
Consider using Unit tests as well to ensure that methods are functioning correctly. You can generate a test cases using Random testing and Equivalence partitioning techniques.
I wouldn't suggest to use Debug.Assert for contract testing, as Debug.Assert is used for development purposes and not meant for checking the contract during testing. As such, the contract that the items remain in respective positions is valid and maintained even after the operations are performed on them.
So, it is not a bug, but an appropriate use of the contract tests
I would recommend you to do some unit tests for all the operations to make sure they are functioning correctly. 
This is what the testing pyramid looks like (Wikipedia):

Bottom: Unit tests validate small, isolated parts of code including function and class interfaces.
Middle: Integration tests validate the interaction between layers of code, like services and repositories, and whether the overall component completes its tasks.
Top: End-to-end/UI or System tests validate the behavior of the software as would a customer or a user see and interact with it, using a UI, an API, or a website.

Your contract tests are closer to the bottom part of the pyramid because they are checking internal state and operations of the system components, rather than external interfaces. Also, contract tests tend to be flaky (i.e., they might have some false failures, which are known as fragile tests) because they only test the parts of the system which are small and isolated.
You could also use Pex for exploring parts of the code that your unit tests haven't touched yet.

Finally, please note that Microsoft Pex does not fully support .NET core or .NET standard libraries. The list of supported types can be found in the Microsoft Pex documentation.

Remember, writing unit tests and contract tests help in testing the behaviour of your code, even while writing testable code. 

A: PEX does well for finding simple or quick checks or contracts, that the count is correct and you get expected results. But when it comes to checking if properties (like whether an element remains in the right spot) do indeed hold true post the operation, PEX may not be sufficient. This is because PEX generates random tests, without accounting for the possible values that a property might hold. 
For instance, when removing an element, PEX might successfully remove an item but may not move all other elements to fill in the gap. This might not be a bug but a valid outcome according to the list's contract. PEX would not be able to catch this kind of error.
Hence, PEX could be a good start to make sure the code is doing what it's supposed to do, but manual testing and thorough unit tests should be performed to ensure accuracy.
Hope this helps and good luck with your code testing!!

A: Contract-based testing is useful to check if the system preserves its well-defined properties upon executing certain operations. But when the operations alter the system's internal state, checking the properties after the operations has no additional value.

In your case, ArrayList.Add always increases the Count by 1, so the well-defined property IndexOf(x) should always return 0 when it adds x. This is a precondition for the method. After the add operation, IndexOf(x) should return a value >=0. This additional condition is not checked in your contract test.

If you want to make the contract test more complete, you have two options:
* Modify the contract test to cover only well-defined preconditions and postconditions without the additional conditions.
* Write equivalent unit tests that cover the additional conditions. The unit test code will be similar to the contract test code, but it won't use PexObserve or Debug.Assert for value logging.
For example, you can write a contract test and--------------------
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf((int)arrList[index]);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf((int)arrList[index]);
            int Old_index = index;
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains((int)arrList[index]);

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf((int)arrList[Old_index]);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf((int)arrList[Old_index]);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains((int)arrList[Old_index]);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

        }
    }
